You’re running into a very common issue when using JMeter’s HTTP(S) Test Script Recorder with applications that use SSO (Single Sign-On).
Let me break down exactly why this happens and how to fix it.
✔️ Why it works without JMeter — but fails with JMeter recorder
When JMeter launches its Proxy Recorder, it uses a self-signed certificate to intercept HTTPS traffic.
Modern SSO systems (Okta, Azure AD, Ping SSO, etc.) block authentication as soon as they detect an untrusted MITM proxy.
In your screenshot, Firefox is saying:
“Warning: Potential Security Risk Ahead — certificate issue.”
This means:
1️⃣ Your browser does not trust the JMeter proxy certificate.
JMeter is effectively acting like a “man-in-the-middle” to record requests. Without trusting its certificate, Firefox blocks the HTTPS traffic—including SSO.
2️⃣ SSO authentication will always fail if the certificate is not trusted.
Because SSO relies heavily on strict HTTPS validation, it refuses to authenticate.
That’s why it doesn’t prompt for username/password normally,
but does during JMeter recording → because the certificate is rejected.
✔️ The Fix — Install the JMeter Root CA certificate into browser
You must install JMeter's certificate into your browser’s trusted store.
Steps:
🔧 Step 1: Start JMeter Recorder
Go to Tools → Recorder (or HTTP(S) Test Script Recorder)
Click Start
JMeter will generate a certificate here:
<JMETER_HOME>/bin/ApacheJMeterTemporaryRootCA.crt
🔧 Step 2: Install Certificate in Firefox
Open Firefox
Go to
Settings → Privacy & Security → Certificates → View Certificates
Go to Authorities
Click Import
Select:
ApacheJMeterTemporaryRootCA.crt
Check:
✔️ Trust this CA to identify websites
🔧 Step 3: Restart the browser and retry JMeter recording
Now Firefox will trust the JMeter proxy and SSO should work like normal.
✔️ Additional Important Notes for SSO Recording
1️⃣ Use Firefox — not Chrome
Chrome blocks local proxy certificates aggressively due to security policies.
Firefox is the browser recommended for JMeter proxy recording.
2️⃣ Some SSO Systems Still Can’t Be Recorded Directly
Depending on SSO technology, some flows cannot be captured via proxy:
SSO Type	Proxy Recording Works?
Form-based login	✔️ Yes
SAML Redirect login	❌ Often No
OAuth / OpenID Login	❌ Usually No
Azure AD / Okta	❌ Typically blocked
If your SSO uses strict security policies, you may need a different approach (I can help you choose):
Options:
✔️ Option A — Bypass SSO using a direct API token from backend
✔️ Option B — Ask dev team for a test login endpoint
✔️ Option C — Use recording after SSO session is established
(log in first normally → then start recorder → do post-login actions)
